  "question": {
    "text": "Rational roots of the equation $2 x^4+x^3-11 x^2+$ $x+2=0$ are",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "ZW4Fh",
      "text": "$\\frac{1}{2}$ and 2",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "m1lYd",
      "text": "$\\frac{1}{2}, 2, \\frac{1}{4},-2$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "u9a33",
      "text": "$\\frac{1}{2}, 2,3,4$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "DMXYv",
      "text": "$\\frac{1}{2}, 2, \\frac{3}{4},-2$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Given equation can be reduced to a quadratic equation.\n$\\therefore 2 x^2+x-11+\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{2}{x^2}=0$\n$\\Rightarrow 2\\left(x^2+\\frac{1}{x^2}\\right)+\\left(x+\\frac{1}{x}\\right)-11=0$\nPut $x+\\frac{1}{x}=y$\n$2\\left(y^2-2\\right)+y-11=0$\n$\\Rightarrow 2 y^2+y-15=0$\n$\\Rightarrow y=-3$ and $\\frac{5}{2}$\n$\\Rightarrow x+\\frac{1}{x}=-3, x+\\frac{1}{x}=\\frac{5}{2}$\n$\\Rightarrow x^2+3 x+1=0,2 x^2-5 x+2=0$\nOnly 2nd equation has rational roots as $\\mathrm{D}=9$ and roots are $\\frac{1}{2}$ and 2 .",
    "image": null
  },
